{"title":"Open set","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb36a0d7cd08ba1869a993a3526f8e9d1129ac3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" /> be a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Metric_space\" title=\"Metric space\">metric space</a>. A subset <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is said to be a <b>open set</b> if, and only if, every point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Interior_point&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Interior point (page does not exist)\">interior point</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. That is, for every point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> we can find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/0/5006802cf6bf472614c4575c33c041a1c0dc113e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r&gt;0 \\in \\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" /> such that every point in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> with a distance less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. There are other definitions for open sets. For a different aproach, search for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Topological_space\" title=\"Topological space\">topological space</a>, where its elements are said to be open sets.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Examples\">Examples</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Open_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Examples\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Every <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Open_interval\" title=\"Open interval\">open interval</a> on the real line is an open set relative to the real line (note that the metric being used is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euclidean_metric\" title=\"Euclidean metric\">Euclidean metric</a>).</li>\n<li>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/3/b437bf52070a68f64a7346e0b7a4043978daa2e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\mathbb{Q}, d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" />) be a metric space, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the Euclidean metric. That being said, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/f/18faac56812975e05744319e3c6ac1016643c11b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"16\" /> is an open set. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/f/18faac56812975e05744319e3c6ac1016643c11b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"16\" /> isn't an open set on the real line, with the Euclidean metric being defined.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Basic_properties\">Basic properties</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Open_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Basic properties\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Theorem.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb36a0d7cd08ba1869a993a3526f8e9d1129ac3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" /> be a metric space, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94ccf2cb2987be894f9c7cee17e7579b923e6df9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\bigcap\\limits^{n}_{i=1} A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"83\" height=\"53\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/b/d2b810443a11bd58a47c18a5eb4b343a26422289.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i \\subset X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" /> is an open set. Under these conditions, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is an open set.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/8/ad8efbf4b3597b9b32633c01b51891bc211ad995.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04a7702dc7d40864e4129617d0d45742e8b7dbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/0/f00340aba36745c22e8873fefef2b76a9f89b8aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i=1,2,...,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"17\" />. So for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> exists <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/0/dd0e9ce8dc4ce7dd290d96736a8f8c97d472f532.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_i &gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"17\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/8/6e850b0e29f3a656d995af82a2c8fc2659b7c25b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{x\\in X| d(x,p)&lt;r_i\\} \\subset A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"207\" height=\"20\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/e/efe6948aef0be4a36f05d30968ad529810b01513.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r = min(r_1, r_2, ..., r_n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"170\" height=\"20\" />. It is clear that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/0/9909030319bc5d01eb515ec0a2c3d615916d99c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B = \\{x \\in X| d(x,p)&lt;r\\} \\subset A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/c/8ecc0cd303619092550f985468e67d0fd70a4204.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i = 1,2, ...,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"17\" />. That implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/b/f2ba185f2dc53ca373802ee3aaf4d28abe92fc67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B\\subset A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is interior to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. That means that every point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is interior to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, as desired. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><b>Theorem.</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/3/bb36a0d7cd08ba1869a993a3526f8e9d1129ac3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(X,d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" /> be a metric space, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/d/77de71e683bfddf9690f6844faea79b6c7af1777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\bigcup\\limits_{k\\in R\\subset \\mathbb{R}} A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"110\" height=\"45\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/5/7553256f94a2ce1069471dfe7c43bf8b7215ab90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"24\" height=\"17\" /> is an open set of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> an arbitrary non-empty subset of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/8/ad8efbf4b3597b9b32633c01b51891bc211ad995.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"47\" height=\"18\" />. So it must exist <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/2/4d2949ebd348fc434c863779178c0d74d0679393.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i, i\\in R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/4/d04a7702dc7d40864e4129617d0d45742e8b7dbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p\\in A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is interior to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/4/2744600189f7f1df31756f2022b2d5212ef1c767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> needs to be interior to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. That means that every point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is interior to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. That proves the theorem. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Look at these results and compare with the axioms of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Topological_space\" title=\"Topological space\">topological space</a>. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Open_set&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Topology\" title=\"Topology\">Topology</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Topological_space\" title=\"Topological space\">Topological space</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Metric_space\" title=\"Metric space\">Metric space</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Limit_point\" title=\"Limit point\">Limit point</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Closed\" title=\"Closed\">Closed</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Open_set&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018102200\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.026 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.067 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 271/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 265/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8896/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.520      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.520      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-